From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Matt Mower <mdmower@cmphys.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 07:38:20 -0800
Subject: [PATCH] [bugs #3979] Initialize every designated member of an
 anonymous struct in a union

The members of an anonymous struct are promoted into the union that has
it. When designators named several of them, one initializer was used.
printIvalStruct() emitted one member of the alternative, with warning
147 when the object itself is the union. createIvalStruct() stopped
after the first designator of a union. `union u v = {.lo = 0x34,
.hi = 0x12}` left hi at 0 for `union u { unsigned int raw; struct {
unsigned char lo, hi; }; }`.

promoteAnonStructs() now marks the first member of each alternative of
a union other than its first one. differentAlternatives() tells from
the marks whether two members are in different alternatives of a union.

printIvalStruct() emits each member that has an initializer in the
alternative that a designator picked. It leaves a member out, as
before, when the list also has an initializer for a member of another
alternative.

createIvalStruct() applies all the leading designators of a union when
they name members of one alternative. For the first alternative, the
members that follow take the initializers without a designator, and
automatic storage has the remaining members zeroed. An initializer in
excess gets warning 147 there. When two designators name members of
different alternatives, the first designator alone is applied, as
before.

Both functions keep to one initializer of the alternative, as before,
for a list in which an initializer can replace a designated one without
the list showing it. nextInitSlot() does not give an initializer
without a designator to a member of a later alternative. In
`{.d = 5, .b = 1, 0, 0}` for `union { int a; struct { char b; union {
char c; char d; }; char e; }; }`, the first 0 is for c and replaces the
initializer of d, and the reordered list has the initializers of b and
d alone. unbraceAnonInit() marks the designated initializer that such
an initializer follows, and reorderIlistFull() marks its list, with the
new member inexact of initList. For a union, createIvalStruct() gives
the initializers without a designator to the members that do not have
one. C gives them to the members behind the last designated one, which
can have an initializer or can be in a union with a designated member
of another alternative. So the first designator alone is applied when
such initializers follow and a designated member is behind the last
designated one.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.2 <noreply@anthropic.com>
